+# Workflow Test Bench (WTB)
+
+[![License](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-Apache%202.0-blue.svg)](https://opensource.org/licenses/Apache-2.0)
+[![Python](https://img.shields.io/badge/python-3.11%2B-blue)](https://www.python.org/)
+[![Build Status](https://img.shields.io/badge/build-passing-brightgreen)](https://github.com/yourusername/wtb)
+
+**Workflow Test Bench (WTB)** is a production-grade framework for testing, debugging, and benchmarking agentic workflows. It bridges the gap between prototype and production by ensuring **transactional integrity**, **reproducibility**, and **observability** for complex AI systems.
+
+> **The Problem:** Modern agentic systems (RAG, autonomous agents) are not just "read-only" chat interfaces. They persist state, modify data, and evolve. Testing them requires more than simple input/output matching—it requires a rig that understands state, side effects, and concurrency.
+
+## ⚡ The Stack
+
+WTB is built on the shoulders of giants, integrating the best-in-class tools for modern Python engineering:
+
+### 🕸️ LangGraph
+**Orchestrate with Confidence.**
+WTB leverages [LangGraph](https://langchain-ai.github.io/langgraph/) for state-native workflow definition. We treat your workflow graph as a first-class citizen, enabling deep introspection, checkpointing, and cyclic execution patterns that are impossible with linear DAGs.
+
+### 🚀 Ray
+**Scale Without Limits.**
+Powered by [Ray](https://www.ray.io/), WTB scales your test execution from a single laptop to a massive cluster. Run thousands of concurrent agent simulations, variant A/B tests, and parameter sweeps with industrial-grade distributed computing capabilities.
+
+### 🐍 uv
+**Lightning-Fast Environments.**
+We mandate [uv](https://github.com/astral-sh/uv) for environment management. WTB ensures that every test run happens in a pristine, reproducible, and isolated environment. No more "it works on my machine" – dependency resolution is instant and deterministic.
+
+### 🧠 AgentGit
+**Version Control for Agents.**
+(Integrated Support) Treat your agent's behavior and prompts as versioned artifacts. WTB integrates with `agentgit` to track, diff, and rollback agent configurations, ensuring that you know exactly *which* version of your agent produced *which* result.
+
+## ✨ Key Features
+
+- **ACID-Compliant Execution**: Guarantees that your workflow state and database side-effects are always consistent, even when agents crash or hallucinate.
+- **Time-Travel Debugging**: Step backward through your agent's reasoning traces. Replay specific checkpoints with different inputs to isolate bugs.
+- **Content-Addressable Artifacts**: All generated files and outputs are hashed and stored immutably.
+- **Variant Testing**: define multiple versions of a node (e.g., "Prompt A" vs "Prompt B") and run them competitively in the same pipeline.
+
+
+note: use RayBatchTestRunner and LangGraphStateAdapter only for deveopment.
+
+## 📦 Installation
+```bash
+# Install basic functions
+uv pip install -e .
+
+# Install with all power features (Node Isolation, Ray)
+uv pip install -e ".[all]"
+```
+
+## 🛠️ Quick Start
+
+A minimal “three-step” example showing how to get started with the **WTB SDK** in under 10 lines of code.
+
+1. **Create a TestBench**
+Uses `WTBTestBench.create(...)` to initialize a local SQLite-backed workspace under `data/`.
+```python
+from wtb.sdk import WTBTestBench
+bench = WTBTestBench.create(
+    mode="development",
+    data_dir="data/"
+)
+```
+
+2. **Register a WorkflowProject**
+Give your workflow a name and upload your graph that builds your project, and register it in `bench`.
+```python
+from wtb.sdk import WorkflowProject
+from examples.wtb_presentation.graphs.unified_graph import create_unified_graph
+project = WorkflowProject(
+    name="core_demo",
+    graph_factory=create_unified_graph  # replace with your graph
+)
+bench.register_project(project)
+```
+
+3. **Run & Inspect**
+Call `bench.run(...)` to execute your graph from a starting state. **AgentGit** will automatically set breakpoints for each run, facilitating subsequent operations.
+```python
+result = bench.run(
+    project=project.name,
+    initial_state={"query": "Hello, world", "messages": []}
+)
+print(f"Execution ID: {result.id}")
+print(f"Status:       {result.status}")
+
+checkpoints = bench.get_checkpoints(result.id)
+print(f"Found {len(checkpoints)} checkpoints")
+```
+
+## 🧠 Next Steps
+Once you've completed the basic setup, here are some next steps you can take to explore more advanced features of the **WTB SDK**.
+
+1. **Checkpoint & Rollback**
+After executing a workflow, you can rollback to any saved checkpoints for state restoration. After executing a workflow, you can rollback to any saved checkpoints, which will restore both the workflow state and file system.
+```python
+target_cp = checkpoints[-1]  # Roll back to the initial checkpoint
+print(f"  - Target checkpoint: {target_cp.id}")
+print(f"  - Target step: {target_cp.step}")
+
+bench.rollback(execution_id=result.id, checkpoint_id=str(target_cp.id))
+state = bench.get_state(result.id)
+print(f"Status: {state}")
+```
+
+2. **Fork for A/B Testing**
+Create forks of your execution from a checkpoint to run different variants, such as for A/B testing.
+```python
+fork_result_A = bench.fork(
+    execution_id=result.id,
+    checkpoint_id=str(checkpoints[0].id),
+    new_initial_state={"query": "Variant A query", "variant": "A"}
+)
+fork_result_B = bench.fork(
+    execution_id=result.id,
+    checkpoint_id=str(checkpoints[0].id),
+    new_initial_state={"query": "Variant B query", "variant": "B"}
+)
+print(f"Fork A Execution ID: {fork_result_A.fork_execution_id[:12]}")
+print(f"Fork B Execution ID: {fork_result_B.fork_execution_id[:12]}")
+```
+
+3. **Batch Testing**
+You can run multiple test cases with different variants in batch testing.Two variants (“default” and ‘bm25’) and two test cases (“Query 1” and “Query 2”) will be tested in batches, with each test run executed 4 times.
+```python
+variant_matrix = [{"retriever": "default"}, {"retriever": "bm25"}]
+test_cases = [{"query": "Test query 1", "messages": []}, {"query": "Test query 2", "messages": []}]
+
+batch_result = bench.run_batch_test(
+    project=project.name,
+    variant_matrix=variant_matrix,
+    test_cases=test_cases
+)
+print(f"Batch Test Results: {len(batch_result.results)} results")
+```
+
+## 🚀 Advanced Features
+
+Up until now, you've learned how to get started with the **WTB SDK** using basic features like **workflow execution**, **checkpointing**, **forking**, and **batch testing**. These capabilities are powerful on their own, but the SDK also supports **advanced features** that enable even more sophisticated workflows and performance optimizations.
+
+1. **Node-Level Environment Isolation**
+With **WTB SDK**, you can configure **per-node virtual environments** to ensure that each node in your workflow has its own isolated Python environment. This isolation helps to avoid dependency conflicts between nodes that may require different versions of libraries.
+```python
+from wtb.sdk import EnvironmentConfig, EnvSpec
+
+environment = EnvironmentConfig(
+    granularity="node",  # Per-node environment isolation
+    uv_manager_url="http://localhost:10900",  # REAL venv service
+    reuse_existing=True,  # Reuse existing environments for speed
+    default_env=EnvSpec(
+        python_version="3.12",
+        dependencies=["openai>=1.0.0", "python-dotenv"],
+    ),
+    node_environments={
+        "rag_load_docs": EnvSpec(
+            python_version="3.12",
+            dependencies=["openai>=1.0.0", "aiofiles"],
+        ),
+        "rag_embed_docs": EnvSpec(
+            python_version="3.12",
+            dependencies=["openai>=1.0.0", "numpy", "faiss-cpu"],
+        ),
+        # Define more environments for other nodes...
+    }
+)
+```
+
+2. **Distributed Execution with Ray**
+Ray is an open-source framework for parallel and distributed computing, and it can be used to scale the execution of workflows in WTB SDK. This is particularly useful when working with large datasets or computationally intensive tasks.
+The following is an example of deploying Ray on a local machine with a quad-core CPU. Before proceeding, make sure to have Ray properly installed and configured, and you need to start the local Ray cluster.
+```bash
+ray start --head
+```
+
+```python
+from wtb.sdk import ExecutionConfig, RayConfig, NodeResourceConfig
+
+execution = ExecutionConfig(
+    batch_executor="ray",  # Use Ray for distributed execution
+    ray_config=RayConfig(
+        address="auto",  # Auto-connect to local Ray cluster
+        max_retries=3,
+    ),
+    node_resources={
+        "rag_load_docs": NodeResourceConfig(num_cpus=1, memory="256MB"),
+        "rag_embed_docs": NodeResourceConfig(num_cpus=2, memory="2GB"),  # Heavy node
+        "rag_generate": NodeResourceConfig(num_cpus=1, memory="1GB"),
+    },
+    checkpoint_strategy="per_node",  # Enable per-node checkpointing
+    checkpoint_storage="sqlite",  # Use SQLite for checkpoint persistence
+)
+```
+
+3. **Workspace Isolation**
+To ensure that each execution runs in a completely isolated environment, workspace isolation can be enabled. This feature ensures that the files used by different executions do not interfere with each other, and each execution gets a clean environment.
+```python
+from wtb.sdk import WorkspaceIsolationConfig
+workspace_isolation=WorkspaceIsolationConfig(
+    enabled=True,
+    mode="copy",  # Copy files for isolation (or use "symlink" to link files)
+)
+```
+
+4. **Pause and Resume Execution**
+In some workflows, you may need to pause the execution after a specific node for human-in-the-loop review or interaction. The pause strategy allows you to pause the workflow at specific points and resume it later.
+```python
+from wtb.sdk import PauseStrategyConfig
+pause_strategy=PauseStrategyConfig(
+    mode="after_node",  # Pause after specific node completion
+)
+```
+
+5. **Create a project with fully unlocked functionality**
+By combining node-level environment isolation, distributed execution with Ray, and workspace isolation, you can ensure that your workflows are both scalable and highly customizable, with fine-grained control over dependencies and resources.
+```python
+project = WorkflowProject(
+    name="wtb_full_presentation",
+    description="Unified RAG + SQL Workflow with Full WTB Features",
+    graph_factory=create_unified_graph,
+    environment=environment,
+    workspace_isolation=workspace_isolation,
+    pause_strategy=pause_strategy
+)
+```
